{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/app at bed3662615a120b3ef4d79aaeac57de5d28afa24: Gitseq's resident is now actually bounded.\n\nThis is the half that was missing, and it is the whole reason the previous head was refused. internal/app constructed kernel.Options with SigningKey, CheckpointProfile and PreAppend and never set MaxQueueDepth, so it defaulted to zero, so the gate never ran, so every Gitseq resident stayed exactly as unbounded as before while the kernel tests passed and the artifact claimed back-pressure had shipped.\n\nThe resident now passes ResidentQueueDepth, a named constant of 32, counting the submission holding the sequencer lock. Zero survives in the kernel as the embedding opt-out; it is not a posture this application takes, and the constant's comment says so.\n\nTestResidentSequencerIsBounded asserts the resident's own constructed depth rather than the kernel primitive. Deleting the option from the construction fails it with \"resident sequencer is unbounded; the queue bound was not passed to the kernel\" — I ran that mutation, because a test written to catch a regression I have already made once should be shown to catch it. A source-text assertion would have been easier and would have proved nothing; this reads the object the resident actually built.\n\nThe test reaches the depth through kernel.Submitter.QueueDepth, which is new. Package app cannot read an unexported kernel field, and the alternative was to assert nothing about the shipped path — which is exactly how the gap shipped last time.\n\nThis rests on the authorizing request, the promise, and 82a33e46, the live artifact for what main carries at this path.","body":{"commit":"bed3662615a120b3ef4d79aaeac57de5d28afa24","path":"internal/app"}}